---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Ahmed Sheta',
  url: new URL('/about/', Astro.site).toString(),
  jobTitle: 'Software and data engineer',
  sameAs: ['https://github.com/Shetta'],
  knowsAbout: ['Distributed systems', 'Data infrastructure', 'Reliability', 'Software architecture']
};
---
<Base title="About" description="About Ahmed Sheta, his engineering work, studies, and public technical notebook." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I'm Ahmed Sheta, a software and data engineer based in Seattle. I grew up in Egypt,
    and my route into engineering was not especially linear.
  </p>
  <p>
    I started in business and analytical work and studied accounting before moving deeper
    into analytics, business intelligence, data engineering, and software engineering.
    My work has included systems at Amazon and AWS. I later studied computer science at
    Georgia Tech, and I continue to study the subjects around it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Much of what I work on now sits between software and infrastructure: distributed
    systems, streaming data, reliability, databases, data platforms, and the operational
    problems that appear once a system has real traffic and real failure modes. I am also
    interested in systems architecture, mathematics, physics, music, and chess.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site is my public technical notebook. I use it to write down architecture
    decisions, production failure modes, experiments, technical questions, and ideas I
    have had to understand carefully enough to explain. Some notes are complete arguments;
    others record work that is still unfinished.
  </p>
  <p>
    I keep the site deliberately plain so the writing lasts, pages load quickly, and code
    remains readable. An article should be useful in a browser, fetched as Markdown,
    indexed by a search engine, or retrieved by a software agent. Every post is available
    through its Markdown URL or with <code>Accept: text/markdown</code>, and
    <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> provides a compact index.
  </p>
</Base>
